{
  "summary": "The round-5 synthesis contains three internal contradictions and two architectural misunderstandings that would cause implementation failures. First, the rejection handling is self-contradictory: it proposes both removing the rejected feature's ID from downstream depends_on lists (cascading cleanup) AND using that same depends_on reference to detect and move downstream features back to plan-inbox for re-planning — you cannot do both, because the cleanup deletes the information the re-planning trigger needs. The correct design is: on rejection, do NOT remove the ID from depends_on; instead, mark the dependency as resolved-via-rejection (is_blocked() treats it as resolved), move downstream features to plan-inbox with a history note, and let the re-planning phase decide whether to remove or replace the dependency. Second, the proposed is_blocked() property and cycle detection both require loading sibling features, but FeatureFile is a single-file data object (constructor takes a path, loads one JSON file) with no reference to its board context or sibling features — calling find() or list_all() from inside a property makes it an expensive I/O operation masquerading as a simple attribute check. Both is_blocked() and cycle detection must be implemented as standalone functions that accept the full feature list as a parameter (e.g., is_blocked(feature, all_features) and detect_cycles(feature, all_features)), called from server.py where the feature list is already loaded, not as FeatureFile instance methods. Third, the WebUI integration proposals misunderstand the Go server's architecture: api.go edit handlers don't directly modify feature files — they relay commands via WebSocket to the connected TUI client, which performs the actual file modification. An edit-depends-on endpoint requires a new WebSocket command type, a TUI-side handler in the message dispatch loop, and the FeatureFile update logic in the TUI process, not just 'a new API endpoint.' Fourth, adding depends_on as an always-persisted field (even empty) on every save() call means any incidental save (description edit, stage move) silently mutates the feature schema in git, producing noisy diffs across hundreds of feature files over time. Instead, only persist depends_on when it is non-empty, and treat absence as equivalent to empty list on read — this keeps existing files untouched until they actually gain dependencies."
}
